WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.020
A white blood cell is carried along in flowing blood, and it must stop where it is

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.720
needed. There, it can stop on the vessel wall in about a second.

3
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:13.620
What switches its grip on?

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.240
A second is too short to make a new protein.

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.840
So the receptor that grips must already sit on the cell surface, switched off.

6
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:24.453
The receptor that grips the wall is an integrin.

7
00:00:25.013 --> 00:00:29.673
It is two different chains, called α and β, and each chain crosses the membrane

8
00:00:29.673 --> 00:00:34.053
once. Their large outer parts together form a head that grips its ligand.

9
00:00:34.653 --> 00:00:37.493
Inside the cell, each chain has only a short tail.

10
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.293
There is no kinase domain and no channel.

11
00:00:41.317 --> 00:00:46.217
In integrins studied most closely, those of platelets and white blood cells, the

12
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:47.757
outer part can fold at a knee.

13
00:00:48.357 --> 00:00:50.277
The head then tips back toward the membrane.

14
00:00:51.197 --> 00:00:54.697
Bent, with the head closed, the integrin holds its ligand weakly.

15
00:00:55.277 --> 00:00:58.797
A white blood cell flowing in the blood keeps its integrins in this form.

16
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.537
So the grip is switched off.

17
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.750
A signal on the vessel wall starts a relay inside the cell.

18
00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.550
The relay helps bring a protein called talin to the integrin tail.

19
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.770
Talin binds the tail of the β chain and separates the two tails.

20
00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:17.490
The change passes through the membrane, the outer part straightens, and the head

21
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.610
opens. Extended like this, the integrin holds its ligand tightly.

22
00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:26.903
The extended integrin grabs its ligand on the vessel wall and holds it tightly.

23
00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:32.363
The cell stops. Nothing had to be made, so the relay can run in about a second.

24
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:36.203
The message started inside the cell and changed the grip outside.

25
00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:38.583
So this is called inside-out signalling.

26
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:42.147
A hormone receptor waits for its ligand.

27
00:01:42.647 --> 00:01:46.967
An integrin is often surrounded by ligand, so the cell controls the receptor

28
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:51.687
instead. So ligand can be all around, and the cell can still keep the grip weak.

29
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:55.687
The control is a change of shape, thrown from inside the cell.

30
00:01:56.327 --> 00:02:00.507
Keep this rule: signals inside the cell can strengthen an integrin's grip.
